WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.155
What makes a conference worth attending?

00:00:03.155 --> 00:00:05.836
Money matters, but so does access.

00:00:05.836 --> 00:00:13.328
This miniature conference lets you explore the trade-off without inventing satisfaction scores.

00:00:13.478 --> 00:00:17.302
Five hundred attendees pay forty each.

00:00:17.302 --> 00:00:20.622
Sponsors contribute ten thousand.

00:00:20.622 --> 00:00:23.942
Total revenue is thirty thousand.

00:00:23.942 --> 00:00:30.382
Costs are twenty-five thousand, leaving a five-thousand surplus.

00:00:30.532 --> 00:00:34.235
There are only two hundred workshop seats.

00:00:34.235 --> 00:00:38.292
That gives forty percent of attendees a place.

00:00:38.292 --> 00:00:44.464
These places show limited access, not how many people want a workshop.

00:00:44.614 --> 00:00:47.324
Add one hundred workshop seats.

00:00:47.324 --> 00:00:49.946
Access rises to sixty percent.

00:00:49.946 --> 00:00:55.803
Costs increase by one thousand, and surplus falls to four thousand.

00:00:55.803 --> 00:00:57.988
Both changes are visible.

00:00:58.138 --> 00:01:02.047
The choice is about priorities and constraints.

00:01:02.047 --> 00:01:12.860
Compare two plans, explore catering and shared space, and remember that sponsor contributions alone do not measure sponsor return.
